@@ -65,6 +65,12 @@ Settings has a Replay history panel that sends the YAML in the editor and a wind
 
 `packeteer -snippet <vendor>` prints the session and filter config for the configured edge and exits. The vendors are `frr`, `bird`, `junos`, `iosxr`, `arista`, and `mikrotik`. The Settings panel shows the same text (`GET /api/snippets?vendor=frr`, admin). The text is generated from the templates in `internal/snippet/templates` and the `asn`, `router_id`, `packeteer_community`, and neighbors in the config; it is never sent to a router. The FRR and BIRD output is loaded by real FRR and BIRD daemons in the `onboarding` CI job.
 
+**Before anything is saved (#569).** The panel does not wait for a saved file. It is written from what is on the page: the text in the YAML editor, with the wizard's ASN, Router ID, Edge address, and flow collector laid over it while the wizard is open (on a box that already has a config the wizard stays closed, and is not read, until you press Run setup again). It sends them to `POST /api/snippets` (admin) as `{"vendor", "yaml", "answers": {"wizard", "asn", "router_id", "edge", "flow_listen"}}`, which reads no file, writes nothing, and sends nothing to a router. After you press Show router config, changing one of those fields or the router redraws it. With an empty editor and an empty wizard it reads the running config (`GET /api/snippets`). While the wizard is open the snippet shows what Generate would save: `asn`, `router_id`, one neighbor (the Edge address), the flow source only when it is ticked, and the community by the wizard's rule (`asn:666` for an AS number up to 65535), whatever the text says. Providers (and so IPv6) are still read from the text. With the wizard closed the text is used as it is.
+
+**Missing answers.** A snippet needs the AS number, the router's address (`bgp.neighbors`), Packeteer's address (`router_id`, or the neighbor's `local_address`), and `packeteer_community`. When any is missing the answer is `422` with `missing`, one plain sentence each that says which wizard step fills it and which file key it is, and the panel lists them. `packeteer -snippet` prints the same sentences. An AS number above 65535 gets no community from the wizard, so set `packeteer_community` as a large community (for example `65536:666:1`).
+
+**Flow export.** When the config has a `flow` source with a `listen` address, the snippet also says where the router sends flow records: the listen address, or Packeteer's address when it listens on every address. Junos gets inline IPFIX sampling, IOS-XR NetFlow v9 with a sampler, Arista sFlow (IPFIX flow tracking on EOS depends on the platform), and RouterOS Traffic Flow v9 ([mikrotik.md](mikrotik.md#traffic-flow-netflow--ipfix)). FRR and BIRD do not export flows; their snippet says so and names the collector ([routers.md](routers.md#flow-export)). A flow source with no `listen` (eBPF only) adds nothing. The sampling rates and interface names in these lines are examples to check against your platform. The snippet holds no secret.
+
 ## Host policy routing
 
 `packeteer -pbr linux` prints the `ip rule` and `ip route` lines for the providers ([policy-routing.md](policy-routing.md)). With `-apply` it compares them with the host and prints the commands that would change; nothing changes until `-yes` is added. After an apply it asks the kernel which way each provider's source leaves and fails if it is not the provider's `next_hop`. A rule is Packeteer's when its priority is 2001 to 2999 and its table is 1001 to 1999; those are removed when their provider is gone; other rules are never touched. In the container this needs `NET_ADMIN`, which the install already grants.
